PR #913  overall: compliant
Spec: Ambiguous spec for CHERI revocation load-side barrier trap conditions and exception priority can cause incorrect hardware implementation, allowing dangling capabilities to escape revocation sweeps and violate temporal memory safety.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:815-828  (inline ISA parser loop, riscv_fill_hwcap_from_isa_string context)
      This code handles the ISA string parser's loop-end logic: skipping '_' separators between multi-letter extensions and continuing on parse errors. It populates a feature bitmap (isa2hwcap) from the ISA string. There is no CHERI, capability, or PTE manipulation anywhere in this code — it is pure CPU feature discovery from device tree / ACPI. No relationship to the security spec (PTE.CRG / sstatus.UCRG load-side trap enforcement).
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:930-952  (inline vendor ext parsing loop, riscv_fill_hwcap_from_isa_string context)
      This code parses vendor-specific ISA extensions from the device tree ('riscv,isa-extensions' property) and populates per-hart vendor ISA bitmaps. It iterates vendor extension lists, checks for subset extensions, and sets bits. This is feature enumeration only — no page tables, no capability loads, no sstatus CSR, no revocation logic. Completely irrelevant to the CHERI PTE.CRG trap spec.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:955-979  riscv_fill_vendor_ext_list
      This function populates the 'all_harts_isa_bitmap' for each vendor by intersecting per-CPU vendor ISA bitmaps. On first CPU, it copies the per-hart bitmap; on subsequent CPUs, it bitwise-ANDs to compute the common subset. This is ISA feature homogeneity tracking. No connection at all to capability loads, PTE.CRG generation bits, sstatus.UCRG, or revocation sweep barriers.

