PR #913  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Ambiguous spec for CHERI revocation load-side barrier trap conditions and exception priority can cause incorrect hardware implementation, allowing dangling capabilities to escape revocation sweeps and violate temporal memory safety.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/pte.h:69-133  (global constants)
      No PTE_CRG (bits 62:61), PTE_CRW (bit 63), or PTE_CD (bit 60) constants are defined. PTE_HI_MASK at line 132 merely marks bits 63:54 as 'reserved for future use'. Svucrg repurposes this reserved field: without these constants, no code in pmap_enter, pmap_fault, or the page-fault handler can initialize or inspect the CRG epoch bits. A page mapped without CRG initialization would carry stale/zero CRG, causing either spurious traps (epoch 0 != current UCRG) or silent acceptance of stale capabilities (if zero is treated as 'no revocation').
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:38-58  (global constants)
      SCAUSE codes cover standard RISC-V exceptions (INST_MISALIGNED through STORE_GUEST_PAGE_FAULT). No CHERI-specific codes for capability load/store traps with CRG mismatch. If Svucrg introduces new SCAUSE codes, the trap dispatchers in trap.c will hit the default: panic() path. If instead CRG-mismatch reuses SCAUSE_LOAD_PAGE_FAULT (13) with additional stval differentiation, the page_fault_handler currently cannot distinguish the two cases and will loop infinitely (see pmap_fault finding). Either approach requires additions here.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:60-80  (global constants)
      SSTATUS bit definitions cover UIE, SIE, UPIE, SPIE, SPP, FS, XS, SUM, SD but not UCRG (user capability revocation generation) or CRGE (capability revocation generation enable). On CHERI hardware, sstatus carries these fields, and they must be saved/restored across traps (exception.S save_registers/load_registers) and context switches. Without the bitmask constants, code cannot mask, set, or clear UCRG/CRGE. The raw 64-bit sstatus read in save_registers will capture the bits implicitly, but no code can inspect or modify them.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:188-195  (global constants)
      Only SATP_MODE_SV39 (8) and SATP_MODE_SV48 (9) are defined. No Svucrg mode encoding. Svucrg extends Sv39/Sv48 with PTE.CRG semantics but may require a distinct SATP mode value or a menvcfg toggle. The SATP_MODE_M mask at line 193 reserves 4 bits (values 0-15), and values 10-15 are unallocated, so a new mode constant is encodable. Without it, the kernel cannot signal to hardware that CRG-aware PTE processing is required.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:104-113  (global constants)
      MSTATUS_VM_MASK and MSTATUS_VM_* constants define the VM mode field in mstatus (MBARE through SV64). No Svucrg entry exists. If Svucrg is enabled via mstatus.VM rather than satp.MODE, a constant must be added here. Note that this is an older mechanism; modern RISC-V uses satp.MODE for VM enable. The MSTATUS_VM constants here may only be relevant for M-mode software, not S-mode FreeBSD.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:203-304  page_fault_handler
      The function dispatches on scause: STORE_PAGE_FAULT -> VM_PROT_WRITE, INST_PAGE_FAULT -> VM_PROT_EXECUTE, else (the catch-all at line 260-262) -> VM_PROT_READ. A CHERI capability load with CRG mismatch arriving as SCAUSE_LOAD_PAGE_FAULT (13) falls into the 'else -> VM_PROT_READ' path. Then at line 264, pmap_fault is called. Since the PTE is valid with correct PTE_R, PTE_U bits (only CRG is stale), pmap_fault returns 1 (success) and the function goto's done without reaching vm_fault_trap. The capability load retries and faults again, creating an infinite loop with no signal delivered to the process. If the CRG mismatch arrives via a new SCAUSE code not in this function's if/else chain, it would be treated as VM_PROT_READ identically, producing the same infinite loop. Either way, the user process hangs indefinitely instead of receiving a SIGSEGV.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:379-460  do_trap_user
      The switch at line 410 dispatches known exception types. If CHERI PTE traps use a new SCAUSE code distinct from the existing page-fault codes, the default case at line 455 fires: 'panic("Unknown userland exception %#lx, trap value %#lx", exception, frame->tf_stval)'. This would crash the kernel on any user process triggering a CRG-mismatch trap — a local DoS. If instead CRG-mismatch traps reuse SCAUSE_LOAD_PAGE_FAULT (the more likely hardware design), the existing dispatch to page_fault_handler at lines 425-428 is taken, but page_fault_handler then exhibits the infinite-loop bug described separately.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:306-377  do_trap_supervisor
